     "The quantized checkpoint is not a deterministic transform of the base model; it is a fit to a calibration distribution, and that distribution is a free parameter that most comparisons never report. Treat it as an experimental input with provenance: record the corpus, its size, its sequence lengths, its language and domain mix, its licensing and privacy status, and the sampling seed, and hash the exact file used. Then test sensitivity rather than assuming insensitivity, by quantizing from at least two materially different calibration corpora and comparing per-slice quality; a large spread between them is direct evidence that the single reported result is one draw from a distribution of possible artefacts. Watch for two specific hazards. Calibration data drawn from the evaluation distribution leaks the benchmark into the artefact and produces an optimistic result that will not hold on production traffic, so calibration and evaluation sources must be disjoint and that disjointness must be verifiable. Calibration sequence lengths far shorter than production's shift the activation statistics that scales are fitted to, which degrades long-context behaviour precisely where it is hardest to notice. If production data is used for calibration, the privacy and retention treatment of the resulting artefact is a compliance question, since fitted scales are derived from that data. ESTIMATE: sensitivity to calibration choice grows as the calibration set shrinks, because the fitted statistics are noisier; derivation is sampling variance of the fitted scale statistics, and it is an ESTIMATE, not MEASURED.",
