     "The objective is lower serving cost, so the comparison must be made on a cost model that matches that objective rather than on a proxy metric. Define cost per token at fixed SLO attainment rather than at peak throughput, since a configuration that maximises tokens per second while missing the latency target is not serving the workload and its cost figure is not comparable. Then add the terms a benchmark table omits: the engineering time to produce and qualify the artefact, the recurring cost of re-qualification triggered by engine, kernel-library and driver upgrades - which recurs on someone else's release schedule, not yours - the storage and distribution cost of maintaining two artefacts, the capacity headroom reserved so a rollback can be absorbed, and the operational cost of a more complex fleet in which not all replicas run the same precision. Amortise the one-off terms over a stated horizon and state the horizon, because a saving that is real per token can still be negative over a quarter if the artefact must be re-qualified twice. Compare against the do-nothing option and the cheap-tuning option on the same model, since those have near-zero recurring qualification cost and therefore a structurally different profile. ESTIMATE: recurring re-qualification cost scales with upgrade frequency times per-qualification effort, so a fast-moving engine can erase a modest per-token saving; derivation is multiplying the recurrence rate by the fixed qualification cost, and it is an ESTIMATE, not MEASURED.",
