     "Performance parity on a clean benchmark says nothing about behaviour at the edges, and quantized arms have edges the baseline does not. Enumerate and exercise them: numerical overflow or NaN propagation in low-precision paths under unusual activation magnitudes; degradation that appears only at long generation lengths as errors accumulate across steps; repetition or degenerate looping that increases output length and therefore cost, which is a cost regression disguised as a quality regression; out-of-memory behaviour under burst when the freed memory has been reallocated to a larger KV pool, leaving less slack than the baseline had; and kernel-level failures on shapes that appear only in rare request patterns. For each, record whether the failure is detected automatically, what the user-visible effect is, how many requests it affects before detection, and how it is contained. Compare containment against the baseline rather than in isolation, because the decision is relative. Include an adversarial and out-of-distribution input set in the evaluation, since quantization shifts decision boundaries and the shifted region is exactly where unusual inputs land. Where a failure mode cannot be exercised in a test environment, say so and record it as residual risk rather than omitting it. ESTIMATE: the number of requests affected before detection scales with the detection interval times the request rate, so alert latency is the dominant lever on blast radius; derivation is rate times time, and it is an ESTIMATE, not MEASURED. Rollback gate: any failure mode with a larger blast radius than the baseline blocks adoption until contained.",
