     "A comparison that ends at adoption leaves the organisation with a one-time measurement and no way to know when it stops being true, and quantized arms drift for reasons the baseline does not share: engine and kernel upgrades change dispatch paths, traffic mix shifts change the decode share, and context-length growth changes the byte ratio the benefit depended on. Instrument the gating metrics themselves rather than proxies, using the same definitions and percentile computation used at qualification so the numbers are comparable across the boundary. Emit precision as an explicit label on every serving metric so dashboards and alerts can separate arms during and after a mixed rollout, which is also what makes an incident attributable. Add continuous quality signals that do not require labels: refusal-rate drift, output-length distribution shift, repetition-rate, and agreement between the deployed arm and a periodically sampled BF16 shadow on a fixed probe set, which is the cheapest direct check that the quality verdict still holds. Define thresholds that page and thresholds that trigger re-qualification, and give the re-qualification trigger an owner and a schedule so it survives staff turnover. Retain per-request records for a stated window, because post-adoption disputes are about specific behaviours that aggregates cannot settle. ESTIMATE: time-to-detect a quality regression is bounded below by the probe interval, so probe frequency directly sets the exposure window; derivation is sampling-interval reasoning, and it is an ESTIMATE, not MEASURED.",
